              %em theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            should be special cases of this, but in practice of course there are many systems called “theories” which are not (yet) as fully formalized as in mathematical logic.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p There are several different viewpoint on theories:
          %ul
            %li
              %p The
              %p
                %a{:href => "#SyntacticView"} syntactic view
              %p
                is that the theory itself consists of a set of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formulas"} formulas
                in the first order language
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Lang
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang(\Sigma)
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variables"} free variables
                (i.e. to be “sentences”), but in weaker logics that lack
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
                it is better to take them to be formulas-in-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"}> context
                \. One also sometimes considers the theory to include all logical consequences (aka theorems) of the axioms in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
                — that is, to be “saturated” with respect to provability.
            %li
              %p The
              %p
                %a{:href => "#SemanticView"} semantic view
              %p
                is that the theory is given by the class of its
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completeness+theorem"} completeness theorem
                is that a sentence in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                is a theorem iff it is satisfied in every model.
            %li
              %p The
              %p
                %a{:href => "#CategoricalView"} categorical view
              %p
                is that the logical formalism of a theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                can frequently be embodied in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
                of terms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{T}}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                are identified with
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{T}} \to Set
              %p
                that preserve some (typically
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} property-like
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structures
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{T}}
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi models
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi F
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi in
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi Set
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{T}} \to \prod_{models\ F\ in\ Set} Set
              %p
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full faithful embedding
                (one that preserves all relevant logical structure). For this reason, completeness theorems are also known as
                = succeed "." do
                  %em embedding theorems
          .query
            %p Hm, is that the way it should be said?
          %p
            In fact, the notion of model can be generalized away from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            to more general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{T}}
            is the generic or universal model for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{T}}
            the theory, then models and theories are placed on the same footing.
          %p In this article we mostly consider the categorical view on “theory”.
          %h3#SyntacticView Syntactic view
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              In first-order
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
              %strong theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              is presented by
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Σ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
              %li
                %p
                  a set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  of
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formula"} formula
                  %strong axioms
                  of the theory: expressed in the first-order language
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Lang
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang(\Sigma)
                  with equality generated by
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            For instance (
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone"}> Johnstone, def. D1.1.6
            ).
          %h3#SemanticView Semantic view
          %p (…)
          %h3#CategoricalView Categorical view
          %p (…)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
          %p (…)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#classes_of_theories Classes of theories
          %p There are many different kinds of “theory” depending on the strength of the “logic”: a by-no-means complete list includes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  equational logic
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/equational+logic"} ?
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+theory"} first-order theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Horn+theory"}> Horn theory
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"}> essentially algebraic theory
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"}> geometric logic
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"}> regular logic
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  exact logic
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/exact+logic"} ?
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  extensive logic
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/extensive+logic"} ?
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+logic"}> coherent logic
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
                (aka pretopos logic),
          %p and corresponding theories for these logics.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+algebraic+theory"} generalized algebraic theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebraic+theory"} commutative algebraic theory
                %li
                  %p
                    finitary algebraic theory:
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermat+theory"} Fermat theory
          %h4#hierarchy_of_theories_cartesian_regular_coherent_geometric Hierarchy of theories: cartesian, regular, coherent, geometric
          %p
            There is a hierarchy of theories that can be interpreted in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a hierarchy of types of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            in the internal logic are represented by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong cartesian theories
                Since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∧
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
                are represented by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
                and identities (top elements), these can be interpreted in any
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lex+category"} lex category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+theory"} cartesian theories
            %li
              %p
                %strong regular theories
                Since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
                is represented by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                of a subobject, it can be interpreted in any
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∧
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
                are
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+theory"} regular theories
            %li
              %p
                %strong coherent theories
                Since
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                are represented by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+elements"} bottom elements
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+theory"} coherent theories
            %li
              %p
                %strong geometric theories
                Finally, theories which also involve infinitary
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+coherent+categories"} infinitary coherent categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+categories"} geometric categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theories"} geometric theories
          %p Note that the axioms of one of these theories are actually of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi \vdash_{\vec{x}} \psi
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            are formulas involving only the specified connectives and quantifiers,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
            means entailment, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{x}
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi φ
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \vec{x}. (\varphi \Rightarrow \psi)
          %p
            so that although
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            are not strictly part of any of the above logics, they can be applied “once at top level.” In an axiom of this form for geometric logic, the formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            which must be built out of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigvee
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
            are sometimes called
            %em positive
            formulas.
          %h3#abelian_theories Abelian theories
          %p
            Interestingly, one form of logic which made an early appearance but is not ordinarily thought of as logic at all is the logic of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            can be thought of as a syntactic site for some “abelian theory”; models of the theory are exact additive functors with domain
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to Ab
          %h3#SpecificExamples Specific examples
          %p The simplest nontrivial theory is the
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+objects"} theory of objects
          %p A still pretty simple but very nontrivial theory is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+function+arithmetic"} elementary function arithmetic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+order+arithmetic"} second order arithmetic
          %p The theories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Th
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Cat
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(Cat)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Th
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Lex
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(Lex)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+categories"} finitely complete categories
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Th
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Topos
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(Topos)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Th
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ETCS
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(ETCS)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"}> ETCS
                )
          %p
            are discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
          %h2#models_for_a_theory Models for a theory
          %h3#settheoretic_models_for_a_firstorder_theory_in_syntactic_approach Set-theoretic models for a first-order theory in syntactic approach
          %p
            The basic concept is of a structure for a first-order language
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            together with an interpretation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a
            %strong model
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            if for every sentence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^M
            is true (“
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            holds in
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is
            %strong consistent
            or satisfiable (relative to the universe in which we do
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (in our universe). Two theories,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            are said to be
            %strong equivalent
            if they have the same models.
          %p
            Given a class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            of structures for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Th
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(K)
            consisting of all sentences in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            which hold in every structure from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            are
            %strong elementary equivalent
            (sometimes written by equality
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M=N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Th
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Th
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(M)=Th(N)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Th
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(K)
            is called a
            %strong set of axioms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %strong finitely axiomatizable
            if there exist a finite set of axioms for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            A theory is said to be
            %strong complete
            if it is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Th
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(M)
            for some structure
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          %h4#categorical_point_of_view_and_models_in_topoi Categorical point of view and models in topoi
          %p
            From the categorical point of view, for every theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            there exists a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            – the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            – such that a model for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T \to \mathcal{T}
            into some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
          %p
            For instance the syntactic categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theories
            are precisely those categories that have finite cartesian
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            to a finite cartesian power
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n
            of a distinguished object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T \to \mathcal{T}
          %p
            We say a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_1 \to \mathcal{T}_2
            of toposes (for instance a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+morphism"} logical morphism
            or a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %em preserves a theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            if for every model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T \to \mathcal{T}_1
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T \to \mathcal{T}_1 \to \mathcal{T}_2
            is a model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_2
          %p
            For instance, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            preserves every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            since, being a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conservative+extension"} conservative extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"}> algebraic theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+theory"} globular theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+presheaf+type"} theory of presheaf type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+analysis"} ordinal analysis
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-theory"}> 2-theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-theory"} 3-theory
          %p
            %br/
          %div
            %p
              %strong mathematical statements
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"}> judgement
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/assertion"} assertion
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypothesis"}> hypothesis
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/consequence"} consequence
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/assignment"} assignment
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definition"} definition
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+definition"}> inductive
                  ,
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinductive+definition"} coinductive
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lemma"} lemma
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"}> proposition
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"}> propositions as types
                  )
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorem"} theorem
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"}> proof
                  \/
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/program"} program
                  (
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proofs+as+programs"}> proofs as programs
                  )
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/example"}> example
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterexample"} counterexample
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"}> conjecture
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/folklore"} folklore
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogy
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tautology"} tautology
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              H. Jerome Keisler,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fundamentals of model theory
          %p
            A standard textbook reference for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            is section D of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
          %p
            A discussion of the relation between theories and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+categories"} syntactic categories
            is at
          %ul
            %li
              n-category cafe 2010:
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/07/what_is_a_theory.html"} what is a theory
          %p Other references include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mamuka+Jibladze"}> Mamuka Jibladze
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Teimuraz+Pirashvili"} Teimuraz Pirashvili
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cohomology of algebraic theories
                %strong 137
                (1991), no. 2, 253–296,
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0021-8693(91)90093-N"} doi
            %li
              %p
                wikipedia:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_first-order_theories"} list of first-order theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morley%27s_categoricity_theorem"} Morley’s categoricity theorem
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decidability_%28logic%29"} Decidability (logic)
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signature_%28logic%29"} signature (logic)
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            theories are specified with the
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gallina+specification+language"} Gallina specification language
              \.
